# D1. The number of //nolint directives in go/. The gate counts them and fails
# on a mismatch, so an exception being added is a two-line diff rather than one
# line nobody sees. Raise it deliberately, with the reason on the line the
# directive sits on.
#
# Phase 1 added four. Three are in internal/jail: the kernel takes a struct
# pointer for landlock_create_ruleset, landlock_add_rule and seccomp, and
# x/sys/unix carries no wrapper for any of the three. The fourth is the escape
# proof re-executing its own binary to build a mount boundary in a private
# namespace, which is the only way that row runs without being root.
#
# Phase 2 added ten. Five are the same shape: SQLite has one integer type and
# it is signed, so a device number, an inode number, a birth time and a byte
# offset cross into it as their bit pattern and come back unchanged. Narrowing
# them would refuse values a real filesystem hands out, which is the opposite
# of what the rule is for.
#
# Four are gosec reading an identifier rather than a value: a constant named
# after the app_password or totp_secret table holds a SQL statement, not a
# credential. The tenth is the import closing databases it only ever read.
#
# Phase 2.5 added two. One is the other direction of the same signed-integer
# crossing: reading a stored device or inode number back out of the override
# table. The other is the source-table inventory's row count, the one statement
# in the tree that names a table rather than binding it, whose name comes from
# that file's own literal map and is checked against a plain-identifier rule
# before it is used.
25
